 app, including unfairly marketing the service to children and teens.\nL93: In January 2026, the Commission announced the claims process through which potentially defrauded consumers could see refunds from the FTC.\nL94: \nL95: Type of Action\nL96: \nL97: Administrative\nL98: \nL99: Last Updated\nL100: \nL101: January 6, 2026\nL102: \nL103: Case Status\nL104: \nL105: Pending\nL106: \nL107: cite2†Press Release L108: ### cite15†FTC Reopens and Sets Aside Rytr Final Order in Response to the Trump Administration’s AI Action Plan L109: \nL110: Date\nL111: \nL112: December 22, 2025\nL113: \nL114: Today, the Federal Trade Commission issued an order to reopen and set aside a 2024 final consent order involving Rytr LLC. The Commission determined after review that the complaint failed to satisfy...\nL115: \nL116: cite13†Cases and Proceedings L117: ### cite16†Rytr LLC, In the Matter of L118: According to the FTC’s complaint, Rytr’s service generated detailed reviews that contained specific, often material details that had no relation to the user’s input, and these reviews almost certainly would be false for the users who copied them and published them online. In many cases, subscribers’ AI-generated reviews featured information that would deceive potential consumers who were using the reviews to make purchasing decisions.\nL119: The complaint further alleges that at least some of Rytr’s subscribers used the service to produce hundreds, and in some cases tens of thousands, of reviews potentially containing false information.\nL120: The proposed order settling the Commission’s complaint is designed to prevent Rytr from engaging in similar illegal conduct in the future. It would bar the company from advertising, promoting, marketing, or selling any service dedicated to – or promoted as – generating consumer reviews or testimonials.\nL121: \nL122: On December 22, 2025, the FTC issued an order to reopen and set aside a 2024 final consent order involving Rytr LLC.\nL123: \nL124: Type of Action\nL125: \nL126: Administrative\nL127: \nL128: Last Updated\nL129: \nL130: December 22, 2025\nL131: FTC Matter/File Number\nL132: \nL133: 232 3052\nL134: \nL135: Case Status\nL136: \nL137: Pending\nL138: \nL139: Report\nL140: ### cite17†6(b) Orders to File Special Report Regarding Advertising, Safety, and Data Handling Practices by Companies Offering Generative Artificial Intelligence (“AI”) Companion Products or Services L141: \nL142: Date\nL143: \nL144: September 11, 2025\nL145: \nL146: File\nL147: \nL148: cite18†Resolution Directing Use of Compulsory Process to Collect Information from Companies that Offer Generative AI Companion Products or Services Regarding Their Advertising, Safety, and Data Handling Practices (96.51 KB)\nL149: \nL150: File\nL151: \nL152: cite19†Model Order to File Special Report (196 KB)\nL153: File\nL154: \nL155: cite20†AI Companion Chatbot Cover Letter (159.21 KB)\nL156: \nL157: cite2†Press Release L158: ### cite21†FTC Launches Inquiry into AI Chatbots Acting as Companions L159: \nL160: Date\nL161: \nL162: September 11, 2025\nL163: \nL164: The Federal Trade Commission is issuing orders to seven companies that provide consumer-facing AI-powered chatbots seeking information on how these firms measure, test, and monitor potentially...\nL165: \nL166: cite2†Press Release L167: ### cite22†FTC Approves Final Order against Workado, LLC, Which Misrepresented the Accuracy of its Artificial Intelligence Content Detection Product L168: \nL169: Date\nL170: \nL171: August 28, 2025\nL172: \nL173: The Federal Trade Commission has given final approval to an order against Workado, LLC, requiring the company to stop advertising the accuracy or efficacy of its artificial intelligence (AI) content...\nL174: \nL175: cite2†Press Release L176: ### cite23†FTC Sues to Stop Air AI from Using Deceptive Claims about Business Growth, Earnings Potential, and Refund Guarantees to Bilk Millions from Small Businesses L177: \nL178: Date\nL179: \nL180: August 25, 2025\nL181: \nL182: The Federal Trade Commission has asked a federal court to stop Air AI from using allegedly deceptive claims about business growth, earnings potential, and refund guarantees to fleece small businesses...\nL183: \nL184: cite2†Press Release L185: ### cite24†FTC Obtains Permanent Ban of E-Commerce Business Opportunity Scheme Operator L186: \nL187: Date\nL188: \nL189: July 30, 2025\nL190: \nL191: The owner of a business opportunity scheme will be permanently banned from selling business opportunities in settlement of Federal Trade Commission allegations that he and his company deceptively...\nL192: \nL193: cite13†Cases and Proceedings L194: ### cite25†FBA Machine/Passive Scaling, FTC v. L195: \nL196: In June 2024, the FTC filed suit against FBA Machine and Bratislav Rozenfeld (also known as Steven Rozenfeld and Steven Rozen) alleging that, in a business opportunity scheme, they falsely guaranteed that consumers could make money operating online storefronts using AI-powered software. The defendants allegedly failed to deliver on the promised earnings claims and defrauded consumers out of over $15 million.\nL197: As a result of the FTC’s complaint, a federal court issued an order temporarily halting the scheme and putting it under the control of a receiver.\nL198: \nL199: The FTC later added Amanda Peremen, Rozenfeld’s wife, as a relief defendant in the case. The amended complaint alleged that, though not directly involved in the scheme, she received proceeds from it.\nL200: In July 2025, the FTC announced that Rozenfeld will be permanently banned from selling business opportunities in settlement of FTC’s allegations and will be required to turn over the contents of multiple financial accounts and any funds realized upon the sale of real estate property. The proceeds will be used for consumer redress.\nL201: \nL202: Type of Action\nL203: \nL204: Federal\nL205: \nL206: Last Updated\nL207: \nL208: July 30, 2025\nL209: \nL210: FTC Matter/File Number\nL211: \nL212: X240032\nL213: \nL214: Case Status\nL215: \nL216: Pending\nL217: \nL218: cite13†Cases and Proceedings L219: ### cite26†Ascend Ecom L220: \nL221: The FTC has filed a lawsuit against an online business opportunity scheme that it alleges has falsely claimed its “cutting edge” AI-powered tools would help consumers quickly earn thousands of dollars a month in passive income by opening online storefronts. According to the complaint, the scheme has defrauded consumers of at least $25 million.\nL222: According to the FTC’s complaint, the operators of the scheme charge consumers tens of thousands of dollars to start online stores on ecommerce platforms such as Amazon, Walmart, Etsy, and TikTok, while also requiring them to spend tens of thousands more on inventory. Ascend’s advertising content claimed the company was a leader in ecommerce, using proprietary software and artificial intelligence to maximize clients’ business success.\nL223: The operators of Ascend Ecom, an online business opportunity that allegedly cost consumers millions of dollars, will be banned from selling business opportunities and required to turn over assets to the Federal Trade Commission under the terms of a proposed court order.\nL224: \nL225: Type of Action\nL226: \nL227: Federal\nL228: \nL229: Last Updated\nL230: \nL231: June 23, 2025\nL232: \nL233: FTC Matter/File Number\nL234: \nL235: 242 3023\nL236: \nL237: Case Status\nL238: \nL239: Pending\nL240: \nL241: Public Statement\nL242: ### cite27†Commissioner Holyoak's Keynote Address at The US AI Summit 2025 L243: \nL244: Date\nL245: \nL246: June 3, 2025\nL247: \nL248: File\nL249: \nL250: cite28†Antitrust and AI: Foundational Principles Meet Foundation Models (248.42 KB)\nL251: \nL252: cite13†Cases and Proceedings L253: ### cite29†Empire Holdings Group LLC, et al. FTC v. L254: The FTC has charged a business opportunity scheme with falsely claiming to help consumers build an “AI-powered Ecommerce Empire” by participating in its training programs that can cost almost $2,000 or by buying a “done for you” online storefront for tens of thousands of dollars. The scheme, known as Ecommerce Empire Builders (EEB), claims consumers can potentially make millions of dollars, but the FTC’s complaint alleges that those profits fail to materialize.\nL255: As a result of the FTC’s complaint, a federal court issued an order temporarily halting the scheme and putting it under the control of a receiver. The FTC’s case against the scheme is ongoing and will be decided by a federal court.\nL256: \nL257: In May 2025, EEB and its owner, Peter Prusinowski (also known as Peter Pru), agreed to a court order that bans them from selling business opportunities and require them to turn over assets to the FTC to be used for refunds to consumers.\nL258: \nL259: Type of Action\nL260: \nL261: Federal\nL262: \nL263: Last Updated\nL264: May 9, 2025\nL265: \nL266: Case Status\nL267: \nL268: Pending\nL269: \nL270: Federal Register Notice\nL271: ### cite30†Workado, LLC, f/k/a Content at Scale AI; Analysis of Proposed Consent Order To Aid Public Comment L272: \nL273: Date\nL274: \nL275: May 2, 2025\nL276: \nL277: Displaying 1 - 20 of 236\nL278: \nL279: cite31†Return to top --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArtificial intelligence | AP News (https://apnews.com/hub/artificial-intelligence)\nciteturn1127view1 [wordlim: 100] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://apnews.com/hub/artificial-intelligence\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2027\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: Menu\nL3: \nL4:   * cite1†World L5: \nL6: SECTIONS\nL7: \nL8: cite2†Iran war cite3†Russia-Ukraine war cite4†Español cite5†China cite6†Asia Pacific cite7†Latin America cite8†Europe cite9†Africa L9: \nL10: TOP STORIES\nL11: \nL12:     * cite10†In defiant speech, Pez